# Code-signing CREDENTIALS — never in a repo, and this one is public.
#
# The distinction that matters: the Team ID and the certificate NAME are
# public by construction. Any distributed app hands them to anyone who
# runs `codesign -dv` on it (Ghostty is 24VZTF6M5V, VS Code is UBF8T346G9),
# so hiding them buys nothing and costs everyone who clones this the
# ability to produce a distributable build. Those stay in project.yml.
#
# What follows is the other half: the PRIVATE KEY and the notarization
# credentials. A leaked .p12 lets a stranger ship malware signed as you,
# and the remedy is revoking the certificate — which invalidates every
# copy already installed. There is no partial recovery.
#
# `notarytool store-credentials` puts its secret in the KEYCHAIN rather
# than a file, which is why the packaging flow uses it instead of passing
# a password on a command line where it would land in shell history.
*.p12
*.pfx
*.p8
*.cer
*.certSigningRequest
*.mobileprovision
*.provisionprofile
# ExportOptions.plist commonly carries an API key id / issuer id.
ExportOptions.plist
# Anything a future script writes while fetching or exporting an identity.
signing/
notarization-*.json
.xcodegen-stamp

# Review transcripts left for the working session, not for the tree.
.claude/audits/
